message: "If you use this hardware, firmware, or documentation, please cite both the paper and the archived release."
title: "LANCET: An open-source programmable thermal-sequencing cartridge platform for sequential multi-step point-of-care biochemistry"
abstract: "Open-source programmable thermal-sequencing cartridge platform that drives arbitrary multi-step point-of-care biochemistry inside a single closed 3D-printed tube. Tunable paraffin-mineral oil wax valves at programmable melt thresholds release reagents in turn under a software-defined thermal protocol delivered by an Arduino-MOSFET controller, at a durable hardware cost of $48.58 USD. The substrate is in principle chemistry-agnostic and supports CRISPR-Cas12a / Cas13, LAMP, sample-preparation cascades, and other multi-step POC chemistries; the demonstrated instantiation reported in the companion HardwareX paper is a dual-threshold valve set (10:7 mass ratio melting at 45 C; 10:8 melting at 50 C) and a five-phase Arduino protocol that runs an RPA-Cas12a workflow for early-window Lyme diagnosis. Only one chemistry has been demonstrated to date."
